                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1003,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 940,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Gives the dot product of two vectors: multiply them element by element and add up - one number that says how much they point the same way.\n\nFor two vectors of length 1 the dot product is the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when they point the same way, 0 at right angles, -1 when opposite. For longer vectors it is scaled by both lengths. With one of them a unit direction, it is the PROJECTION: how far the other reaches along that direction - the component of a movement along an axis, of a force along a surface, of a pose along a learned direction.\n\nReach for it to compare directions (similarity), to project onto an axis, or to test for right angles (dot 0).\n\nFOR ARRAYS OF MORE THAN ONE DIMENSION:\n2-D with 2-D is matrix multiplication, the same as np.matmul. A set of vectors (N rows of 3) with one vector of 3 gives N dot products - every row against the same direction at once.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.dot\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1;\nnothing is sent until it has. The lengths must match (for 2-D, the columns of in 1 must match the rows of in 2), or an error is printed and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndot product:\nA float for two vectors; an array for the larger cases.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nin 1 is the vector a, which starts as [1 0], pointing along x; drag its numbers. in 2 is b, a vector of length 1 at the angle on the slider (make_direction turns the angle into [cos, sin]). The dot product reads 1 at 0 degrees, 0 at 90, -1 at 180 - the cosine of the angle between them. Make a longer, [2 0], and every reading doubles. 'b' goes through 't bang array': the array is stored in in 2 first, then the bang makes a re-send, so turning the slider updates the answer.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.inner is the same for vectors but pairs rows with rows for 2-D arrays. np.matmul is the matrix product. np.linalg.norm gives a length (the square root of a vector dotted with itself). np.cross gives the vector at right angles to both. t.cosine_similarity gives the cosine directly, whatever the lengths.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 924,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Gives the dot product of two vectors: multiply them element by element and add up - one number that says how much they point the same way.\n\nFor two vectors of length 1 the dot product is the cosine of the angle between them: 1 when they point the same way, 0 at right angles, -1 when opposite. For longer vectors it is scaled by both lengths. With one of them a unit direction, it is the PROJECTION: how far the other reaches along that direction - the component of a movement along an axis, of a force along a surface, of a pose along a learned direction.\n\nReach for it to compare directions (similarity), to project onto an axis, or to test for right angles (dot 0).\n\nFOR ARRAYS OF MORE THAN ONE DIMENSION:\n2-D with 2-D is matrix multiplication, the same as np.matmul. A set of vectors (N rows of 3) with one vector of 3 gives N dot products - every row against the same direction at once.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.dot\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1;\nnothing is sent until it has. The lengths must match (for 2-D, the columns of in 1 must match the rows of in 2), or an error is printed and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndot product:\nA float for two vectors; an array for the larger cases.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nin 1 is the vector a, which starts as [1 0], pointing along x; drag its numbers. in 2 is b, a vector of length 1 at the angle on the slider (make_direction turns the angle into [cos, sin]). The dot product reads 1 at 0 degrees, 0 at 90, -1 at 180 - the cosine of the angle between them. Make a longer, [2 0], and every reading doubles. 'b' goes through 't bang array': the array is stored in in 2 first, then the bang makes a re-send, so turning the slider updates the answer.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.inner is the same for vectors but pairs rows with rows for 2-D arrays. np.matmul is the matrix product. np.linalg.norm gives a length (the square root of a vector dotted with itself). np.cross gives the vector at right angles to both. t.cosine_similarity gives the cosine directly, whatever the lengths."
                },
